      <h2 id="bigger-picture">The bigger picture: From AI capability to commercial value</h2>
      <p>When I look at the AI market, I see enormous opportunity. But I also see a need for more disciplined thinking about how businesses develop, position and commercialise AI.</p>
      <p>The organisations that succeed will not necessarily be the ones building the most technology or making the biggest claims. They will be the ones that understand their customers, solve meaningful problems, build solutions that work in real environments and create a credible route to market.</p>
      <p>They will know what they stand for, why their product is different, how they will reach customers and how they will measure success.</p>
      <p>That is why I believe AI leadership needs to connect the entire business lifecycle:</p>
      <div class="pullquote">PAD → Innovation POD → POC → POR<br><br>With brand positioning and GTM developing from the beginning, and stakeholder reporting and governance providing accountability across every stage.</div>
      <p>Innovation without implementation is experimentation. Implementation without commercial strategy is an investment without a clear route to return.</p>
      <p>And neither delivers its full potential without accountability and measurable outcomes.</p>
      <p>My approach has always been to start with the business problem, understand what needs to change and then determine where AI can make a meaningful difference.</p>
      <div class="closing">
        <h2>AI is not the business strategy.</h2>
        <p class="lead">The business outcome is the strategy. AI is one of the tools that can help deliver it.</p>
        <p>If you are developing an AI product, exploring agentic AI or trying to move from experimentation to measurable business value, start with the problem, the readiness and the commercial outcome.</p>
